description:
addressbook.rs:322-384. The onSuccessSetIsDefault match block:

    match on_success_set_is_default {
        Some(Value::String(id_str)) => { ... },
        Some(Value::Null) | None => {}
        _ => {} // malformed — silently ignored
    }

clippy::pedantic flags this with match_same_arms: the two empty arms collapse to a single `_ => {}`. Keeping them split is defensible (the comment "malformed — silently ignored" documents the third case as distinct from the legitimate-null case), but the idiomatic Rust pattern would express this with an exhaustive enum match and a dedicated helper enum like `OnSuccessSetIsDefault::{Apply(Id), NoOp, Malformed}` rather than splitting JSON Value patterns.

Engineering cost: zero (the code is correct). The finding is a marker for the stringly-typed-deserialization smell: deserializing the JMAP arg as serde_json::Value and pattern-matching on Value::String / Value::Null / catchall is what you do when you have not lifted the per-method arg into a typed struct. The mail-server canonical extension args (e.g. EmailSubmission/set onSuccessUpdateEmail) likely have the same shape; lifting to a typed `OnSuccess` enum across the workspace is a separate proposal — file there if accepted.

This is the natural place to flag the under-typed wire-arg parsing in /set handlers as a workspace-wide idiom concern. The current code works; it just is not the idiomatic way to consume a JMAP method's args.
